# IntraCore: параметры HAL блока из базы STM32CubeMX (db/mcu/IP/*_Modes.xml).
# Порождено tools/hal_import — руками не править. Формат: src/Docs/Description/HalCatalogFormat.md
[Ip]
Name=FREERTOS
Version=H7RS-v8.0.0_Cube

[Parameters]
# имя|подпись|группа|единица|мин|макс|описание
configUSE_NEWLIB_REENTRANT|USE_NEWLIB_REENTRANT|Newlib settings (see parameter description first)||||(from https://freertos.org/a00110.html) If configUSE_NEWLIB_REENTRANT is set to 1 then a newlib reent structure will be allocated for each created task. Note Newlib support has been included by popular demand, but is not used by the FreeRTOS maintainers themselves. FreeRTOS is not responsible for resulting newlib operation. User must be familiar with newlib and must provide system-wide implementations of the necessary stubs. Be warned that (at the time of writing) the current newlib design implements a system-wide malloc() that must be provided with locks. <b>Warning: </b> if enabled without using newlib, CubeMx project compilation will fail ("cannot open source file "reent.h")
copyHeapFile|Use FW pack heap file|Project settings (see parameter description first)||||If <b>Enabled</b> (default value), copies the heap file (defined in "Memory Management scheme") from the FW pack to the generated project. If <b>Disabled</b>, it is up to the user to provide a file with, at least, vPortFree() and pvPortMalloc() implementations inside (they will otherwise be missing at linking time).
FreeRTOS_API|FreeRTOS API|API||||Shows which API is used: CMSIS v1, CMSIS v2 or Native.
CMSIS_version|CMSIS-RTOS version|Versions||||
FREERTOS_version|FreeRTOS version|Versions||||
RAM_SIZE|RAM_SIZE|||||
MAX_RAM_SIZE|MAX RAM SIZE|||||
MAX_SIZE_IN_USE|Max RAM size according to memory allocation|||||
MIN_PRIO_IN_USE|Min Prio used by freertos tasks (middleware ones and user ones)|||||
configUSE_PREEMPTION|USE_PREEMPTION|Kernel settings||||Set to 1 to use the preemptive RTOS scheduler, or 0 to use the cooperative RTOS scheduler.
configCPU_CLOCK_HZ|CPU_CLOCK_HZ|Kernel settings||||This must be set to the frequency of the clock that drives the peripheral used to generate the kernels periodic tick interrupt. This is very often, but not always, equal to the main system clock frequency.
configTICK_RATE_HZ|TICK_RATE_HZ|Kernel settings||1|1000|Sets the tick interrupt frequency. The value is specified in Hz. <b>Warning: </b> The max value is 1000, as higher values generate compilation errors (division by zero)
configMAX_PRIORITIES|MAX_PRIORITIES|Kernel settings||||Sets the maximum priority that can be assigned to a task. Tasks can be assigned a priority from zero, which is the lowest priority, to (MAX_PRIORITIES-1), which is the highest priority. <b>Warning: </b> Set to 56 when CMSIS-RTOS v2 is in use.
configUSE_SB_COMPLETED_CALLBACK|USE_SB_COMPLETED_CALLBACK|Kernel settings||0|1|When set to 1, APIs for message buffer can be used to create a stream buffer or message buffer instance with application provided callback overrides. When the option is set to 0,then the default callbacks as defined by sbSEND_COMPLETED() and sbRECEIVE_COMPLETED() macros are invoked. <b>Warning: </b> The functionality is currently not supported for MPU enabled ports.
configUSE_MINI_LIST_ITEM|USE_MINI_LIST_ITEM|Kernel settings||0|1|when set to 1, ListItem_t and MiniLitItem_t remain separate types. However, when the option is set to 0, MiniLitItem_t and ListItem_t are both typedefs of the same struct xLIST_ITEM.
configMINIMAL_STACK_SIZE|MINIMAL_STACK_SIZE|Kernel settings|Words|64|=MAX_SIZE_IN_USE|Sets the size of the stack allocated to the idle task. The value is specified in words (here, of 32 bits), not bytes. <b>Warning: </b> The value chosen by the user should take into account the number of threads, the total heap size and the system stack size. If the totality of used stack size is higher than the total heap size, FreeRTOS will not be able to create and manage the tasks. For information: max value = configTOTAL_HEAP_SIZE/4 (when allocation is dynamic) max value = MCU ram size/4 (when allocation is static)
configMAX_TASK_NAME_LEN|MAX_TASK_NAME_LEN|Kernel settings||12|255|Sets the maximum number of characters that can be used for the name of a task. The NULL terminator is included in the count of characters.
configUSE_16_BIT_TICKS|USE_16_BIT_TICKS|Kernel settings||||The tick count is held in a variable of type portTickType. When USE_16_BIT_TICKS is set to 1 (Enabled), portTickType is defined to be an unsigned 16-bit type. When USE_16_BIT_TICKS is set to 0 (Disabled), portTickType is defined to be an unsigned 32-bit type. Using a 16-bit type can greatly improve efficiency on 8-bit and 16-bit microcontrollers, but at the cost of limiting the maximum block time that can be specified. <b>Warning: </b> Forced to 0 (Disabled) as the STM32 architecture is a 32 bit one.
configIDLE_SHOULD_YIELD|IDLE_SHOULD_YIELD|Kernel settings||||IDLE_SHOULD_YIELD controls the behavior of the idle task if there are application tasks that also run at the idle priority. It only has an effect if the preemptive scheduler is being used. - if IDLE_SHOULD_YIELD is set to 0, then the idle task will never yield to another task, and will only leave the Running state when it is preempted. - if IDLE_SHOULD_YIELD is set to 1, then the idle task will never perform more than one iteration of its defined functionality without yielding to another task if there is another idle priority task that is in the Ready state. This ensures a minimum amount of time is spent in the idle task when application tasks are available to run.
configUSE_MUTEXES|USE_MUTEXES|Kernel settings||||Forced to 1 when CMSIS_RTOS_V2 is used (constraint due to current cmsis_os2.c file in FW packs).
configUSE_RECURSIVE_MUTEXES|USE_RECURSIVE_MUTEXES|Kernel settings||||Forced to 1 when CMSIS_RTOS_V2 is used (constraint due to current cmsis_os2.c file in FW packs).
configUSE_COUNTING_SEMAPHORES|USE_COUNTING_SEMAPHORES|Kernel settings||||Set to 1 to include counting semaphore functionality in the build, or 0 to omit counting semaphore functionality from the build. Forced to 1 when CMSIS_RTOS_V2 is used (constraint due to current cmsis_os2.c file in FW packs).
configQUEUE_REGISTRY_SIZE|QUEUE_REGISTRY_SIZE|Kernel settings||0|255|The queue registry has two purposes, both of which are associated with RTOS kernel aware debugging: 1.It allows a textual name to be associated with a queue for easy queue identification within a debugging GUI. 2.It contains the information required by a debugger to locate each registered queue and semaphore. The queue registry has no purpose unless you are using a RTOS kernel aware debugger.
configUSE_APPLICATION_TASK_TAG|USE_APPLICATION_TASK_TAG|Kernel settings||||USE_APPLICATION_TASK_TAG must be defined as 1 for the vTaskSetApplicationTaskTag function to be available. This function is intended for advanced users only. A 'tag' value can be assigned to each task. This value is for the use of the application only - the RTOS kernel itself does not make use of it in any way. The FreeRTOS trace macros documentation page provides a good example of how an application might make use of this feature.
configTOTAL_HEAP_SIZE|TOTAL_HEAP_SIZE|Kernel settings|Byte(s)|1|=1024*($RamSize-1)|Useless, hidden, memory allocation = static
configHEAP_CLEAR_MEMORY_ON_FREE|HEAP_CLEAR_MEMORY_ON_FREE|Memory management settings||0|1|When set in FreeRTOSConfig.h, the memory freed using vPortFree() is automatically cleared to zero.
HEAP_NUMBER|Memory Management scheme|Memory management settings||||The RTOS kernel allocates RAM each time a task, queue, mutex, software timer or semaphore is created. To replace and improve the use of C standard libray malloc() and free() functions, the FreeRTOS download includes four sample memory allocation implementations. The schemes are implemented in the heap_1.c, heap_2.c, heap_3.c, heap_4.c and heap5.c sources files respectively. The schemes defined by heap_1.c, heap_2.c, heap_4.c and heap_5.c allocate memory from a statically allocated array, known as the FreeRTOS heap. TOTAL_HEAP_SIZE sets the size of this array. The size is specified in bytes.
configGENERATE_RUN_TIME_STATS|GENERATE_RUN_TIME_STATS|Run time and task stats gathering related definitions||||FreeRTOS can be configured to collect task run time statistics. The vTaskGetRunTimeStats() API function formats the collected run time statistics into a human readable table. GENERATE_RUN_TIME_STATS must be set to 1 (Enabled) for vTaskGetRunTimeStats() to be available. <b>Note:</b> when set to 1, two empty functions are generated in the freertos.c file (to be completed by the user)
configUSE_TRACE_FACILITY|USE_TRACE_FACILITY|Run time and task stats gathering related definitions||||Set to 1 (Enabled) if you wish to include additional structure members and functions to assist with execution visualization and tracing. Forced to 1 (as configGENERATE_RUN_TIME_STATS==1, configUSE_STATS_FORMATTING_FUNCTIONS==1 and configSUPPORT_DYNAMIC_ALLOCATION==1), as required by tasks.c
configUSE_STATS_FORMATTING_FUNCTIONS|USE_STATS_FORMATTING_FUNCTIONS|Run time and task stats gathering related definitions||||Set USE_TRACE_FACILITY and USE_STATS_FORMATTING_FUNCTIONS to 1 (Enabled) to include the vTaskList() and vTaskGetRunTimeStats() functions in the build. Setting either to 0 (Disabled) will omit vTaskList() and vTaskGetRunTimeStates() from the build.
configUSE_IDLE_HOOK|USE_IDLE_HOOK|Hook function related definitions||||The idle task hook function is a hook (or callback) function that, if defined and configured, will be called by the idle task on each iteration of its implementation. - if USE_IDLE_HOOK is set to 1 (Enabled) then the application must define an idle task hook function: <b>void vApplicationIdleHook(void)</b>. - if USE_IDLE_HOOK is set to 0 (Disabled) then the idle task hook function will not be called, even if one is defined. <b>Note:</b> when set to 1, an empty function is generated in the freertos.c file (to be completed by the user)
configUSE_TICK_HOOK|USE_TICK_HOOK|Hook function related definitions||||The tick hook function is a hook (or callback) function that, if defined and configured, will be called during each tick interrupt. - if USE_TICK_HOOK is set to 1 (Enabled) then the application must define a tick hook function: <b>void vApplicationTickHook(void)</b>. - if USE_TICK_HOOK is set to 0 (Disabled) then the tick hook function will not be called, even if one is defined. <b>Note:</b> when set to 1, an empty function is generated in the freertos.c file (to be completed by the user)
configUSE_MALLOC_FAILED_HOOK|USE_MALLOC_FAILED_HOOK|Hook function related definitions||||The malloc() failed hook function is a hook (or callback) function that, if defined and configured, will be called if pvPortMalloc() ever returns NULL. - NULL will be returned only if there is insufficient FreeRTOS heap memory remaining for the requested allocation to succeed. If USE_MALLOC_FAILED_HOOK is set to 1 (Enabled) then the application must define a malloc() failed hook function: <b>void vApplicationMallocFailedHook(void)</b>. If USE_MALLOC_FAILED_HOOK is set to 0 (Disabled) then the malloc() failed hook function will not be called, even if one is defined. <b>Note:</b> when set to 1, an empty function is generated in the freertos.c file (to be completed by the user)
configCHECK_FOR_STACK_OVERFLOW|CHECK_FOR_STACK_OVERFLOW|Hook function related definitions||||FreeRTOS provides two optional mechanisms that can be used to assist in stack overflow detection and debugging. - when CHECK_FOR_STACK_OVERFLOW is set to 1 (option1), one mechanism is used - when CHECK_FOR_STACK_OVERFLOW is set to 2 (option2), both mechanisms are used. If CHECK_FOR_STACK_OVERFLOW is not set to 0 (Disabled), the application must provide a stack overflow hook (or callback) function: <b>void vApplicationStackOverflowHook( xTaskHandle xTask, signed char *pcTaskName );</b> The kernel will call that stack overflow hook whenever a stack overflow is detected <b>Note:</b> when set to 1 or 2, a function with an empty body is generated in the freertos.c file (to be completed by the user)
configUSE_CO_ROUTINES|USE_CO_ROUTINES|Co-routine related definitions||||Set to 1 to include co-routine functionality in the build, or 0 to omit co-routine functionality from the build. To include co-routines croutine.c must be included in the project.
configMAX_CO_ROUTINE_PRIORITIES|MAX_CO_ROUTINE_PRIORITIES|Co-routine related definitions||1|255|Sets the maximum priority that can be assigned to a co-routine. Co-routines can be assigned a priority from zero, which is the lowest priority to (MAX_CO_ROUTINE_PRIORITIES-1), which is the highest priority.
configUSE_TIMERS|USE_TIMERS|Software timer definitions||||Set to 1 (Enabled) to include software timer functionality. <b>Dependency:</b> Forced to 1 (Enabled) when - CMSIS_RTOS_V2 is used (constraint due to current cmsis_os2.c file in FW packs) - xTaskGetSchedulerState equals to 0 (Disabled) - xTimerPendFunctionCall equals to 1 (Enabled) - or xEventGroupSetBitFromISR equals to 1 (Enabled).
configTIMER_TASK_PRIORITY|TIMER_TASK_PRIORITY|Software timer definitions||0|=configMAX_PRIORITIES-1|Sets the priority of the software timer service/daemon task.
configTIMER_TASK_STACK_DEPTH|TIMER_TASK_STACK_DEPTH|Software timer definitions|Words|=configMINIMAL_STACK_SIZE|=configTOTAL_HEAP_SIZE/4|Sets the size of the stack (in words, not bytes) allocated to the timer service task.
configTIMER_QUEUE_LENGTH|TIMER_QUEUE_LENGTH|Software timer definitions||1|255|Sets the length of the software timer command queue.
configLIBRARY_LOWEST_INTERRUPT_PRIORITY|LIBRARY_LOWEST_INTERRUPT_PRIORITY|Interrupt nesting behaviour configuration||1|15|The lowest interrupt priority that can be used in a call to a "set priority" function.
configLIBRARY_MAX_SYSCALL_INTERRUPT_PRIORITY|LIBRARY_MAX_SYSCALL_INTERRUPT_PRIORITY|Interrupt nesting behaviour configuration||1|3|The highest interrupt priority that can be used by any interrupt service routine that makes calls to interrupt safe FreeRTOS API functions. DO NOT CALL INTERRUPT SAFE FREERTOS API FUNCTIONS FROM ANY INTERRUPT THAT HAS A HIGHER PRIORITY THAN THIS! (higher priorities are lower numeric values).
configPRIO_BITS|PRIO_BITS|Cortex-M specific definitions||0|8|
INCLUDE_vTaskPrioritySet|vTaskPrioritySet|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the vTaskPrioritySet() API function, set vTaskPrioritySet to 1 (Enabled) To exclude it from your build, set vTaskPrioritySet to 0 (Disabled)
INCLUDE_uxTaskPriorityGet|uxTaskPriorityGet|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the uxTaskPriorityGet() API function, set uxTaskPriorityGet to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set uxTaskPriorityGet to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_vTaskDelete|vTaskDelete|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the vTaskDelete() API function, set vTaskDelete to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set vTaskDelete to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_vTaskCleanUpResources|vTaskCleanUpResources|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the vTaskCleanUpResources() API function, set vTaskCleanUpResources to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set vTaskCleanUpResources to 0 (Disabled). <b>Note:</b>
INCLUDE_vTaskSuspend|vTaskSuspend|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the vTaskSuspend() API function, set vTaskSuspend to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set vTaskSuspend to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_vTaskDelayUntil|vTaskDelayUntil|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the vTaskDelayUntil() API function, set vTaskDelayUntil to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set vTaskDelayUntil to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_vTaskDelay|vTaskDelay|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the vTaskDelay() API function, set vTaskDelay to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set vTaskDelay to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_xTaskGetSchedulerState|xTaskGetSchedulerState|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the xTaskGetSchedulerState() API function, set xTaskGetSchedulerState to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set xTaskGetSchedulerState to 0 (Disabled).
configUSE_ALTERNATIVE_API|USE_ALTERNATIVE_API|Kernel settings||||Set to 1 to include the 'alternative' queue functions in the build, or 0 to omit the 'alternative' queue functions from the build. The alternative API is described within the queue.h header file. <b>Note:</b> The alternative API was deprecated some time ago, and was removed in FreeRTOS V9.0 0.
INCLUDE_xTaskResumeFromISR|xTaskResumeFromISR|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the xTaskResumeFromISR() API function, set xTaskResumeFromISR and vTaskSuspend to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set xTaskResumeFromISR or vTaskSuspend to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_xQueueGetMutexHolder|xQueueGetMutexHolder|Include definitions||||Configuration constant introduced in FreeRTOS v7.2.0 that is used as the default value of xSemaphoreGetMutexHolder (if it is not explicitly set)
INCLUDE_xSemaphoreGetMutexHolder|xSemaphoreGetMutexHolder|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the xSemaphoreGetMutexHolder() API function, set xSemaphoreGetMutexHolder and USE_MUTEXES to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set xSemaphoreGetMutexHolder or USE_MUTEXES to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_pcTaskGetTaskName|pcTaskGetTaskName|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the pcTaskGetTaskName() API function, set pcTaskGetTaskName to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set pcTaskGetTaskName to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark|uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark() API function, set uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle|xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle() API function, set xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_eTaskGetState|eTaskGetState|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the eTaskGetState() API function, set eTaskGetState to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set eTaskGetState to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_xEventGroupSetBitFromISR|xEventGroupSetBitFromISR|Include definitions||||Set bits (flags) within an RTOS event group. A version of xEventGroupSetBits() that can be called from an interrupt service routine (ISR). <b>Note:</b> INCLUDE_xTimerPendFunctionCall must be set to 1 to make the xEventGroupSetBitFromISR() function available.
INCLUDE_xTimerPendFunctionCall|xTimerPendFunctionCall|Include definitions||||Used to pend the execution of a function to the RTOS daemon task (the timer service task, hence this function is pre-fixed with 'Timer'). <b>Note:</b> configUSE_TIMERS must be set to 1 to make the xTimerPendFunctionCall() function available
configENABLE_BACKWARD_COMPATIBILITY|ENABLE_BACKWARD_COMPATIBILITY|Kernel settings||||The FreeRTOS.h header file includes a set of #define macros that map the names of data types used in versions of FreeRTOS prior to version 8.0.0 to the names used in FreeRTOS version 8.0.0. The macros allow application code to update the version of FreeRTOS they are built against from a pre 8.0.0 version to a post 8.0.0 version without modification. Setting configENABLE_BACKWARD_COMPATIBILITY to 0 in FreeRTOSConfig.h excludes the macros from the build, and in so doing allowing validation that no pre version 8.0.0 names are being used.
configUSE_PORT_OPTIMISED_TASK_SELECTION|USE_PORT_OPTIMISED_TASK_SELECTION|Kernel settings||||Some FreeRTOS ports have two methods of selecting the next task to execute - a generic method, and a method that is specific to that port. The Generic method: - Is used when configUSE_PORT_OPTIMISED_TASK_SELECTION is set to 0, or when a port specific method is not implemented. - Can be used with all FreeRTOS ports. - Is completely written in C, making it less efficient than a port specific method. - Does not impose a limit on the maximum number of available priorities. A port specific method: - Is not available for all ports. - Is used when configUSE_PORT_OPTIMISED_TASK_SELECTION is set to 1. - Relies on one or more architecture specific assembly instructions (typically a Count Leading Zeros [CLZ] of equivalent instruction) so can only be used with the architecture for which it was specifically written. - Is more efficient than the generic method. - Typically imposes a limit of 32 on the maximum number of available priorities.
configUSE_TICKLESS_IDLE|USE_TICKLESS_IDLE|Kernel settings||||By setting the configUSE_TICKLESS_IDLE, the idle task suppresses ticks and the processor stays in a low power mode for as long as possible. - when set to 1 (Built in functionality enabled), two macros, configPRE_SLEEP_PROCESSING and configPOST_SLEEP_PROCESSING are generated in FreeRTOSConfig.h, and two empty functions, PreSleepProcessing and PostSleepProcessing generated in freertos.c (to be completed with user code). - when set to 2 (User defined functionality enabled), TO COMPLETE...
configUSE_TASK_NOTIFICATIONS|USE_TASK_NOTIFICATIONS|Kernel settings||||Each RTOS task has a 32-bit notification value. An RTOS task notification is an event sent directly to a task that can unblock the receiving task, and optionally update the receiving task's notification value. RTOS task notification functionality is enabled by default, and can be excluded from a build (saving 8 bytes per task) by setting configUSE_TASK_NOTIFICATIONS to 0 in FreeRTOSConfig.h (achieved when setting USE_TASK_NOTIFICATIONS to Disabled). When disabling USE_TASK_NOTIFICATIONS, USE_OS2_THREAD_FLAGS also needs to be disabled to avoid error compilations.
configOVERRIDE_DEFAULT_TICK_CONFIGURATION|OVERRIDE_DEFAULT_TICK_CONFIGURATION|Kernel settings||||Must be set to 1 if the application writer wants to provide their own implementation of vPortSetupTimerInterrupt().
MEMORY_ALLOCATION|Memory Allocation|Memory management settings||||FreeRTOS versions prior to V9.0.0 allocate the memory used by the RTOS objects listed below from the special FreeRTOS heap. FreeRTOS V9.0.0 and onwards gives the application writer the ability to instead provide the memory themselves, allowing the following objects to optionally be created without any memory being allocated dynamically: Tasks, Software Timers, Queues, Event Groups, Semaphores and Mutexes. For that purpose, two new parameters are available: configSUPPORT_DYNAMIC_ALLOCATION and configSUPPORT_STATIC_ALLOCATION. (see http://www.freertos.org/Static_Vs_Dynamic_Memory_Allocation.html for more information). In CubeMx, setting MEMORY_ALLOCATION allows to configure configSUPPORT_STATIC_ALLOCATION and configSUPPORT_DYNAMIC_ALLOCATION parameters at the same time: - when equals to "Dynamic", sets configSUPPORT_DYNAMIC_ALLOCATION to 1 and configSUPPORT_STATIC_ALLOCATION to 0 - when equals to "Static", sets configSUPPORT_DYNAMIC_ALLOCATION to 0 and configSUPPORT_STATIC_ALLOCATION to 1 - when equals to "Dynamic / Static", sets both configSUPPORT_DYNAMIC_ALLOCATION and configSUPPORT_STATIC_ALLOCATION to 1 <b>Note:</b> Forced to 2 (Dynamic / Static), constraint due to the current CMSIS-RTOS V2 files (mainly cmsis_os2.c).
configUSE_DAEMON_TASK_STARTUP_HOOK|USE_DAEMON_TASK_STARTUP_HOOK|Hook function related definitions||||If USE_TIMERS and USE_DAEMON_TASK_STARTUP_HOOK are both set to 1 (Enabled) then the application must define an hook function: <b>void vApplicationDaemonTaskStartupHook(void).</b> <b>Note:</b> when set to 1, an empty function is generated in the freertos.c file (to be completed by the user)
INCLUDE_xTaskAbortDelay|xTaskAbortDelay|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the xTaskAbortDelay() API function, set xTaskAbortDelay to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set xTaskAbortDelay to 0 (Disabled).
INCLUDE_xTaskGetHandle|xTaskGetHandle|Include definitions||||It is one of the macros allowing those components of the real time kernel not utilized by your application to be excluded from your build. - this ensures the RTOS does not use any more ROM or RAM than necessary for your particular embedded application. To include the xTaskGetHandle() API function, set xTaskGetHandle to 1 (Enabled). To exclude it from your build, set xTaskGetHandle to 0 (Disabled).
configRECORD_STACK_HIGH_ADDRESS|RECORD_STACK_HIGH_ADDRESS|Kernel settings||||When set to 1 (Enabled) the stack start address is saved into each task's TCB (assuming stack grows down).
configENABLE_MPU|ENABLE_MPU|MPU/FPU||||Use or not Memory Protection Unit with FreeRTOS. Very first step of implementation on WB for Examples Migration. To be consolidated on F4.
configENABLE_FPU|ENABLE_FPU|MPU/FPU||||Use or not FPU with FreeRTOS.
configMESSAGE_BUFFER_LENGTH_TYPE|MESSAGE_BUFFER_LENGTH_TYPE|Added with 10.2.1 support||||Defaults to size_t for backward compatibility, but can be overridden in FreeRTOSConfig.h if lengths will always be less than the number of bytes in a size_t.
configUSE_POSIX_ERRNO|USE_POSIX_ERRNO|Added with 10.2.1 support||||Description to be completed.
INCLUDE_uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark2|uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark2|Include definitions||||Description to be completed.
configUSE_OS2_THREAD_SUSPEND_RESUME|USE_OS2_THREAD_SUSPEND_RESUME|CMSIS-RTOS V2 flags||||When set to Disabled, it will exclude CMSIS-RTOS2 functions osThreadSuspend and osThreadResume from the application image.
configUSE_OS2_THREAD_ENUMERATE|USE_OS2_THREAD_ENUMERATE|CMSIS-RTOS V2 flags||||When set to Disabled, it will exclude CMSIS-RTOS2 function osThreadEnumerate from the application image.
configUSE_OS2_EVENTFLAGS_FROM_ISR|USE_OS2_EVENTFLAGS_FROM_ISR|CMSIS-RTOS V2 flags||||When set to Disabled, it will disable CMSIS-RTOS2 function osEventFlagsSet and osEventFlagsClear operation from ISR.
configUSE_OS2_THREAD_FLAGS|USE_OS2_THREAD_FLAGS|CMSIS-RTOS V2 flags||||When set to Disabled, it will exclude CMSIS-RTOS2 Thread Flags API functions from the application image.
configUSE_OS2_TIMER|USE_OS2_TIMER|CMSIS-RTOS V2 flags||||When set to Disabled, it will exclude CMSIS-RTOS2 Timer API functions from the application image.
configUSE_OS2_MUTEX|USE_OS2_MUTEX|CMSIS-RTOS V2 flags||||When set to Disabled, it will exclude CMSIS-RTOS2 Mutex API functions from the application image.
FirstAddedTab|First additional tab|||||
SecondAddedTab|Second additional tab|||||
TasksTableTitle|Title for the tasks table|||||
TasksColumns|Names for the table columns|||||
MaxTasks|Maximum number of tasks|||||
TasksKeys|Keys for config file|||||
Tasks01|Tasks (1 to 15)|||||
Tasks02|Tasks (16 to ..)|||||
